            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Measures a common error in Shadow takes: a good T-pose, but once the arms come down they hang to one side - both arms, together. A body hangs its arms as mirror images, so however far each one splays out, the pair is centred on the chest. hang_symmetry measures how far the pair has shifted to one side, the same way for both: not how vertical the arms are, and not each arm's splay.\n\nIt reads the frames where both upper arms are well below level and at about the same height - all of them, or only those in the ranges you give. Its loss is ready for t.optimize, to learn shadow_mag_correct's 'hang' weights; add it to elbow_hinge's with + when learning both.\n\nSYNTAX:\nhang_symmetry\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntake in:\nShadow quaternions, (frames, 37 or 20 joints, 4), scalar first. A tensor carrying gradients keeps them.\n\nranges:\nAn option: frame ranges to use, like 10430-11242, 3767-4000 - stretches where the dancer's arms hang symmetrically (from the video). Empty uses every frame where both arms hang.\n\nevery:\nAn option: if the take was thinned (t[] ::6), the step, so the ranges still name frames of the full take.\n\nbelow level:\nAn option, in degrees (50): how far below level both upper arms must hang.\n\nrobust width:\nAn option, in degrees (4). Misses smaller than this count by their square, larger ones only in proportion - frames where the choreography itself is asymmetric pull little.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nloss:\nOne number for t.optimize; smaller is better.\n\nshift:\nThe typical shared shift, in degrees: positive toward the dancer's left.\n\nframes used:\nHow many frames it read.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA made-up take of both arms hanging, swaying in and out together: as mirror images (0), then with both shifted 15 degrees toward the dancer's right (-15).\n\nRELATED:\nshadow_mag_correct's 'hang' term is the correction; elbow_hinge is the loss for the elbows. The full setup on a real take is examples/shadow_mag_learning.json.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Measures a common error in Shadow takes: a good T-pose, but once the arms come down they hang to one side - both arms, together. A body hangs its arms as mirror images, so however far each one splays out, the pair is centred on the chest. hang_symmetry measures how far the pair has shifted to one side, the same way for both: not how vertical the arms are, and not each arm's splay.\n\nIt reads the frames where both upper arms are well below level and at about the same height - all of them, or only those in the ranges you give. Its loss is ready for t.optimize, to learn shadow_mag_correct's 'hang' weights; add it to elbow_hinge's with + when learning both.\n\nSYNTAX:\nhang_symmetry\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntake in:\nShadow quaternions, (frames, 37 or 20 joints, 4), scalar first. A tensor carrying gradients keeps them.\n\nranges:\nAn option: frame ranges to use, like 10430-11242, 3767-4000 - stretches where the dancer's arms hang symmetrically (from the video). Empty uses every frame where both arms hang.\n\nevery:\nAn option: if the take was thinned (t[] ::6), the step, so the ranges still name frames of the full take.\n\nbelow level:\nAn option, in degrees (50): how far below level both upper arms must hang.\n\nrobust width:\nAn option, in degrees (4). Misses smaller than this count by their square, larger ones only in proportion - frames where the choreography itself is asymmetric pull little.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nloss:\nOne number for t.optimize; smaller is better.\n\nshift:\nThe typical shared shift, in degrees: positive toward the dancer's left.\n\nframes used:\nHow many frames it read.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA made-up take of both arms hanging, swaying in and out together: as mirror images (0), then with both shifted 15 degrees toward the dancer's right (-15).\n\nRELATED:\nshadow_mag_correct's 'hang' term is the correction; elbow_hinge is the loss for the elbows. The full setup on a real take is examples/shadow_mag_learning.json."
        },
